margin-left:4.5pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify'>&lt;span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>                Historical backgrounds are important
because they &lt;u>illuminate&lt;/u> scripture. In addition, they often &lt;u>confirm&lt;/u>
the biblical record and occasionally may even help &lt;u>prove&lt;/u> its
trustworthiness in regard to things that can be tested. Consequently, we tend
to view historical backgrounds favorably as a valuable resource. This
presentation, however, stresses another observation. Like all sources of
information, historical background, misused, can harm rather than help biblical
studies. In the wrong hands, it can be invoked in ways that detract from
hermeneutical and apologetic concerns.&lt;/span>&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>HEBREWS AND THE ALEXANDRIAN ORIGIN OR DESTINY</title><link>https://christir.org/essays/evidences/critical-intro-nt/hebrews/hebrews-and-the-alexandrian-origin-or-destiny/</link><pubDate>Thu, 02 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://christir.org/essays/evidences/critical-intro-nt/hebrews/hebrews-and-the-alexandrian-origin-or-destiny/</guid><description>&lt;style>
